From 8519c7571ea9509ad43dd8d932425f9f692a4d02 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: LegendaryFire <tristan.balon@outlook.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 18:48:48 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Read Inkling 2's prompts faster: a warp-specialized NVFP4
 expert GEMM

The prompt's expert GEMM ran each expert's routed rows in 64 x 128
tiles, every warp copying, converting and multiplying in turn, a hot
expert's row tiles (up to ~2000 rows of a 1024-token chunk, the mean
64) one after another in a single block. Now:

- the row tiles of every expert are a list the grid walks
  (k_tiles), so a hot expert's rows spread over many blocks;
- NVFP4 runs k_expert_ws: 8 warps copy the rows and convert a step's
  weights to FP16 while 8 others multiply the step before (64 x 128
  tiles, 32 x 32 a warp), handing the buffers over with named
  barriers (rows three steps deep, converted weights two); the raw
  weights are copied four steps at a time in 16-byte pieces (8 for
  the 648-byte down rows), and the block scale is decoded without
  branches;
- the codes go through a byte-to-half2 table, the rows' K permuted
  within each 16 (a byte's two nibbles side by side), shared memory
  swizzled instead of padded.

Q8_0 experts keep k_expert_mma2 (now with the tile list). The K
order within 16 changes the tensor cores' rounding: the 20K text's
ppl 3.941 -> 3.875 (this model's rounding noise; every kernel here
gives the same output as the others).

Two cards, both executors, the DSpark drafter (expert GEMM time on
the busier card -10%): 7K 1679 -> 1818 tok/s, 60K 1739 -> 1865, 180K
1428 -> 1508. Decode unchanged.
